1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,485
The Titanic was the largest moving object

2
00:00:03,485 --> 00:00:05,270
ever built by humans.

3
00:00:05,450 --> 00:00:08,748
It had sixteen watertight compartments,

4
00:00:08,748 --> 00:00:12,299
electric doors that closed with one switch

5
00:00:12,299 --> 00:00:13,483
on the bridge,

6
00:00:13,483 --> 00:00:17,711
and a reputation for being practically unsinkable.

7
00:00:17,891 --> 00:00:20,173
It hit an iceberg at eleven forty

8
00:00:20,173 --> 00:00:22,455
at night, and two hours and forty

9
00:00:22,455 --> 00:00:24,322
minutes later, it was gone.

10
00:00:24,502 --> 00:00:26,604
The hole was not even big.

11
00:00:26,784 --> 00:00:29,628
Today, ten parts of the Titanic,

12
00:00:29,628 --> 00:00:32,382
and how each one helped a giant

13
00:00:32,382 --> 00:00:33,982
ship sink so fast.

14
00:00:34,333 --> 00:00:38,225
The Titanic left Southampton on April tenth,

15
00:00:38,225 --> 00:00:41,675
nineteen twelve, on its first voyage to

16
00:00:41,675 --> 00:00:42,471
New York.

17
00:00:42,651 --> 00:00:45,671
It was two hundred sixty-nine meters long,

18
00:00:45,671 --> 00:00:48,260
about eight hundred eighty-two feet,

19
00:00:48,260 --> 00:00:50,921
and it carried more than two thousand

20
00:00:50,921 --> 00:00:52,287
two hundred people.

21
00:00:52,467 --> 00:00:55,576
Its builders, Harland and Wolff in Belfast,

22
00:00:55,576 --> 00:00:58,396
were among the best shipbuilders in the

23
00:00:58,396 --> 00:00:58,830
world.

24
00:00:59,010 --> 00:01:01,273
And that is what makes the story

25
00:01:01,273 --> 00:01:02,051
so strange.

26
00:01:02,231 --> 00:01:04,436
Almost every part we will look at

27
00:01:04,436 --> 00:01:05,773
today was well made.

28
00:01:05,953 --> 00:01:08,294
The problem was how the parts worked

29
00:01:08,294 --> 00:01:10,051
together on one cold night.

30
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:13,132
As always, every fact comes from a

31
00:01:13,132 --> 00:01:16,908
named source, the official British and American

32
00:01:16,908 --> 00:01:21,166
inquiries, metallurgy studies of steel recovered from

33
00:01:21,166 --> 00:01:23,977
the wreck, and maritime historians,

34
00:01:23,977 --> 00:01:26,387
all listed in the description.

35
00:01:26,567 --> 00:01:28,538
Let's start with the walls that were

36
00:01:28,538 --> 00:01:29,632
supposed to save it.

37
00:01:29,982 --> 00:01:33,307
Why didn't the Titanic's watertight walls save

38
00:01:33,307 --> 00:01:33,524
it?

39
00:01:33,704 --> 00:01:36,959
The hull was divided by fifteen steel

40
00:01:36,959 --> 00:01:38,982
walls called bulkheads,

41
00:01:38,982 --> 00:01:41,622
creating sixteen compartments.

42
00:01:41,802 --> 00:01:44,177
The ship could stay afloat with any

43
00:01:44,177 --> 00:01:45,873
two compartments flooded,

44
00:01:45,873 --> 00:01:48,384
and even with the first four flooded.

45
00:01:48,564 --> 00:01:51,393
But the iceberg opened the first five,

46
00:01:51,393 --> 00:01:52,732
and maybe a sixth.

47
00:01:52,912 --> 00:01:54,886
And here is the fatal detail.

48
00:01:55,066 --> 00:01:57,276
The bulkheads did not go all the

49
00:01:57,276 --> 00:01:57,760
way up.

50
00:01:57,940 --> 00:02:00,237
Most of them stopped at E deck,

51
00:02:00,237 --> 00:02:02,978
only a few decks above the waterline.

52
00:02:03,328 --> 00:02:06,370
So the compartments were not sealed boxes.

53
00:02:06,550 --> 00:02:08,310
They were more like an ice cube

54
00:02:08,310 --> 00:02:08,594
tray.

55
00:02:08,774 --> 00:02:10,876
As the bow filled with water and

56
00:02:10,876 --> 00:02:13,307
sank lower, the water reached the top

57
00:02:13,307 --> 00:02:15,738
of a bulkhead and simply spilled over

58
00:02:15,738 --> 00:02:17,446
into the next compartment.

59
00:02:17,626 --> 00:02:18,485
Then the next.

60
00:02:18,665 --> 00:02:19,617
Then the next.

61
00:02:19,797 --> 00:02:21,163
The ship designer,

62
00:02:21,163 --> 00:02:24,577
Thomas Andrews, walked through the damage and

63
00:02:24,577 --> 00:02:27,611
told the captain the Titanic would sink.

64
00:02:27,791 --> 00:02:29,876
He gave it about an hour or

65
00:02:29,876 --> 00:02:31,344
an hour and a half.

66
00:02:31,693 --> 00:02:34,027
Why build the walls so low?

67
00:02:34,207 --> 00:02:37,322
Higher bulkheads would have cut through the

68
00:02:37,322 --> 00:02:38,699
first class spaces,

69
00:02:38,699 --> 00:02:41,524
the long corridors and the grand public

70
00:02:41,524 --> 00:02:43,263
rooms that sold tickets.

71
00:02:43,443 --> 00:02:45,324
And the rules of the time did

72
00:02:45,324 --> 00:02:46,427
not require more.

73
00:02:46,607 --> 00:02:48,018
After the disaster,

74
00:02:48,018 --> 00:02:51,213
Titanic's sister ship Olympic was sent back

75
00:02:51,213 --> 00:02:52,402
to the shipyard,

76
00:02:52,402 --> 00:02:55,374
and several of its bulkheads were raised

77
00:02:55,374 --> 00:02:55,895
higher.

78
00:02:56,075 --> 00:02:57,491
The fix was easy.

79
00:02:57,671 --> 00:02:59,447
It just came too late.

80
00:02:59,797 --> 00:03:02,202
Why did the Titanic have a double

81
00:03:02,202 --> 00:03:02,712
bottom?

82
00:03:02,892 --> 00:03:05,574
Because the most common danger for a

83
00:03:05,574 --> 00:03:07,735
big ship was running aground,

84
00:03:07,735 --> 00:03:10,194
tearing the bottom open on rocks.

85
00:03:10,374 --> 00:03:13,105
So the Titanic had a second steel

86
00:03:13,105 --> 00:03:16,001
skin inside the bottom of the hull,

87
00:03:16,001 --> 00:03:18,236
with a gap of about one and

88
00:03:18,236 --> 00:03:19,394
a half meters,

89
00:03:19,394 --> 00:03:21,380
a little over five feet,

90
00:03:21,380 --> 00:03:22,704
between the two.

91
00:03:22,884 --> 00:03:24,910
If the outer bottom was ripped open,

92
00:03:24,910 --> 00:03:26,711
the inner one would keep the sea

93
00:03:26,711 --> 00:03:26,936
out.

94
00:03:27,116 --> 00:03:30,576
Against rocks, it was excellent protection.

95
00:03:30,925 --> 00:03:32,659
But the iceberg did not hit the

96
00:03:32,659 --> 00:03:33,050
bottom.

97
00:03:33,230 --> 00:03:35,433
It scraped along the side of the

98
00:03:35,433 --> 00:03:37,154
bow, below the waterline.

99
00:03:37,334 --> 00:03:39,968
And the sides of the Titanic were

100
00:03:39,968 --> 00:03:41,803
a single skin of steel,

101
00:03:41,803 --> 00:03:44,916
about two and a half centimeters thick,

102
00:03:44,916 --> 00:03:46,193
around one inch.

103
00:03:46,373 --> 00:03:49,581
Interestingly, the idea of a double skin

104
00:03:49,581 --> 00:03:51,586
on the sides was not new.

105
00:03:51,766 --> 00:03:54,680
The giant steamship Great Eastern had a

106
00:03:54,680 --> 00:03:57,745
double hull back in eighteen fifty-eight.

107
00:03:57,925 --> 00:03:59,267
It was expensive,

108
00:03:59,267 --> 00:04:00,294
it was heavy,

109
00:04:00,294 --> 00:04:02,743
and most liners did not use it.

110
00:04:03,092 --> 00:04:04,697
After the Titanic,

111
00:04:04,697 --> 00:04:05,856
that changed.

112
00:04:06,036 --> 00:04:08,843
Olympic was refitted with an inner skin

113
00:04:08,843 --> 00:04:11,218
along the sides of its boiler and

114
00:04:11,218 --> 00:04:12,154
engine rooms.

115
00:04:12,334 --> 00:04:16,089
Today, oil tankers must have double hulls

116
00:04:16,089 --> 00:04:19,569
by law, after other disasters like the

117
00:04:19,569 --> 00:04:21,309
Exxon Valdez spill.

118
00:04:21,489 --> 00:04:23,933
The lesson took decades to spread,

119
00:04:23,933 --> 00:04:25,227
but it was simple.

120
00:04:25,407 --> 00:04:27,514
Protect the ship from the side it

121
00:04:27,514 --> 00:04:28,855
actually gets hit on.

122
00:04:29,205 --> 00:04:32,262
Why do some researchers blame tiny rivets

123
00:04:32,262 --> 00:04:33,977
for the Titanic's fate?

124
00:04:34,157 --> 00:04:36,902
The hull plates were held together by

125
00:04:36,902 --> 00:04:38,906
about three million rivets.

126
00:04:39,086 --> 00:04:41,170
In the middle of the ship,

127
00:04:41,170 --> 00:04:42,453
they were steel,

128
00:04:42,453 --> 00:04:45,100
driven by big hydraulic machines.

129
00:04:45,280 --> 00:04:47,369
But at the bow and the stern,

130
00:04:47,369 --> 00:04:50,108
where the hull curves and the machines

131
00:04:50,108 --> 00:04:51,116
could not fit,

132
00:04:51,116 --> 00:04:53,494
workers hammered them in by hand,

133
00:04:53,494 --> 00:04:56,088
and many of those were wrought iron.

134
00:04:56,268 --> 00:04:58,405
And the bow is exactly where the

135
00:04:58,405 --> 00:04:59,206
iceberg hit.

136
00:04:59,555 --> 00:05:01,258
In two thousand eight,

137
00:05:01,258 --> 00:05:05,283
metallurgists Jennifer Hooper McCarty and Tim Foecke

138
00:05:05,283 --> 00:05:08,379
studied rivets recovered from the wreck.

139
00:05:08,559 --> 00:05:10,921
Some had high amounts of slag,

140
00:05:10,921 --> 00:05:14,701
glassy impurities that make wrought iron weaker.

141
00:05:14,881 --> 00:05:17,719
Their theory is that the iceberg pushed

142
00:05:17,719 --> 00:05:19,102
against the plates,

143
00:05:19,102 --> 00:05:21,140
the rivet heads snapped off,

144
00:05:21,140 --> 00:05:24,051
and the seams between plates opened like

145
00:05:24,051 --> 00:05:24,633
zippers.

146
00:05:24,813 --> 00:05:27,565
That would explain why the damage was

147
00:05:27,565 --> 00:05:30,243
a series of thin openings instead of

148
00:05:30,243 --> 00:05:31,210
one big gash.

149
00:05:31,560 --> 00:05:33,332
Not every expert agrees,

150
00:05:33,332 --> 00:05:35,915
and the rivets were not unusual for

151
00:05:35,915 --> 00:05:36,727
their time.

152
00:05:36,907 --> 00:05:39,514
But the wreck supports one part of

153
00:05:39,514 --> 00:05:40,204
the idea.

154
00:05:40,384 --> 00:05:43,128
When the bow was surveyed with sonar

155
00:05:43,128 --> 00:05:45,033
in the nineteen nineties,

156
00:05:45,033 --> 00:05:48,692
investigators found the damage was mostly narrow

157
00:05:48,692 --> 00:05:50,369
slits along the seams.

158
00:05:50,549 --> 00:05:52,403
At the British inquiry,

159
00:05:52,403 --> 00:05:56,675
the shipbuilder's naval architect estimated the total

160
00:05:56,675 --> 00:06:00,061
opening at only around twelve square feet,

161
00:06:00,061 --> 00:06:01,915
about one square meter.

162
00:06:02,095 --> 00:06:04,951
A ship of forty-six thousand tons,

163
00:06:04,951 --> 00:06:07,556
sunk through a hole the size of

164
00:06:07,556 --> 00:06:08,144
a door.

165
00:06:08,493 --> 00:06:11,485
Why might the Titanic's steel have cracked

166
00:06:11,485 --> 00:06:12,197
so easily?

167
00:06:12,377 --> 00:06:16,615
Because steel changes its personality with temperature.

168
00:06:16,795 --> 00:06:19,581
Warm steel bends before it breaks.

169
00:06:19,761 --> 00:06:21,654
Cold steel can shatter.

170
00:06:21,834 --> 00:06:24,220
The sea that night was about minus

171
00:06:24,220 --> 00:06:25,624
two degrees Celsius,

172
00:06:25,624 --> 00:06:27,870
twenty-eight degrees Fahrenheit,

173
00:06:27,870 --> 00:06:30,677
colder than freezing because it was salt

174
00:06:30,677 --> 00:06:31,098
water.

175
00:06:31,278 --> 00:06:33,016
In the nineteen nineties,

176
00:06:33,016 --> 00:06:36,421
scientists tested steel recovered from the wreck,

177
00:06:36,421 --> 00:06:39,062
and at that temperature it was brittle

178
00:06:39,062 --> 00:06:41,286
compared with modern ship steel.

179
00:06:41,636 --> 00:06:43,471
The reason was the chemistry.

180
00:06:43,651 --> 00:06:45,660
The steel had a lot of sulfur

181
00:06:45,660 --> 00:06:47,877
and relatively little manganese,

182
00:06:47,877 --> 00:06:49,540
and it was made in a way

183
00:06:49,540 --> 00:06:51,757
that left it more brittle in the

184
00:06:51,757 --> 00:06:52,103
cold.

185
00:06:52,283 --> 00:06:54,605
That was normal for nineteen twelve.

186
00:06:54,785 --> 00:06:58,582
Nobody understood brittle fracture well until the

187
00:06:58,582 --> 00:06:59,900
Second World War,

188
00:06:59,900 --> 00:07:02,922
when some Liberty ships cracked in half

189
00:07:02,922 --> 00:07:04,007
in cold water,

190
00:07:04,007 --> 00:07:06,332
a few of them while sitting in

191
00:07:06,332 --> 00:07:06,720
port.

192
00:07:07,070 --> 00:07:10,970
Today, ship steel is tested for exactly

193
00:07:10,970 --> 00:07:11,470
this.

194
00:07:11,650 --> 00:07:14,892
Engineers hit a small notched sample with

195
00:07:14,892 --> 00:07:17,739
a heavy pendulum at low temperature,

196
00:07:17,739 --> 00:07:19,400
called a Charpy test,

197
00:07:19,400 --> 00:07:21,931
to prove it will bend instead of

198
00:07:21,931 --> 00:07:22,563
shatter.

199
00:07:22,743 --> 00:07:25,719
Ships built for icy waters must pass

200
00:07:25,719 --> 00:07:27,620
at colder temperatures.

201
00:07:27,800 --> 00:07:30,705
The Titanic was built with the best

202
00:07:30,705 --> 00:07:32,200
steel of its time.

203
00:07:32,380 --> 00:07:34,656
It was just tested by the coldest

204
00:07:34,656 --> 00:07:35,898
water of its life.

205
00:07:36,248 --> 00:07:38,982
Why couldn't the Titanic turn away from

206
00:07:38,982 --> 00:07:39,824
the iceberg?

207
00:07:40,004 --> 00:07:42,341
This is its identical twin,

208
00:07:42,341 --> 00:07:45,112
Olympic, built right next to it.

209
00:07:45,292 --> 00:07:46,440
Look at the tall,

210
00:07:46,440 --> 00:07:48,195
thin rudder in the middle.

211
00:07:48,375 --> 00:07:51,119
It was about seventy-eight feet tall,

212
00:07:51,119 --> 00:07:54,457
twenty-four meters, and weighed more than one

213
00:07:54,457 --> 00:07:55,422
hundred tons.

214
00:07:55,602 --> 00:07:56,859
That sounds huge,

215
00:07:56,859 --> 00:07:59,300
but compared with the size of the

216
00:07:59,300 --> 00:08:00,780
ship, it was narrow.

217
00:08:00,960 --> 00:08:03,059
It met the rules of the time,

218
00:08:03,059 --> 00:08:05,230
and it was designed for a ship

219
00:08:05,230 --> 00:08:07,980
that would cross the ocean in straight

220
00:08:07,980 --> 00:08:09,934
lines, not dodge obstacles.

221
00:08:10,114 --> 00:08:12,497
A big liner was built to be

222
00:08:12,497 --> 00:08:14,085
stable, not agile.

223
00:08:14,435 --> 00:08:17,192
The lookouts saw the iceberg only about

224
00:08:17,192 --> 00:08:19,950
thirty-seven seconds before the impact.

225
00:08:20,130 --> 00:08:23,385
First Officer William Murdoch ordered the helm

226
00:08:23,385 --> 00:08:25,579
hard over, and the bow began to

227
00:08:25,579 --> 00:08:26,004
swing.

228
00:08:26,184 --> 00:08:27,183
In that time,

229
00:08:27,183 --> 00:08:30,949
the Titanic, traveling at about twenty-two knots,

230
00:08:30,949 --> 00:08:33,869
covered more than four hundred meters,

231
00:08:33,869 --> 00:08:35,867
about a quarter of a mile.

232
00:08:36,047 --> 00:08:38,336
The ship had started to turn,

233
00:08:38,336 --> 00:08:40,705
and that is exactly why it did

234
00:08:40,705 --> 00:08:41,968
not hit head-on.

235
00:08:42,148 --> 00:08:44,191
It scraped along the side.

236
00:08:44,541 --> 00:08:47,290
And here is one of the cruelest

237
00:08:47,290 --> 00:08:48,976
ideas in the story.

238
00:08:49,156 --> 00:08:52,149
Many naval architects believe that a head-on

239
00:08:52,149 --> 00:08:54,870
collision would have crushed the bow and

240
00:08:54,870 --> 00:08:57,318
flooded maybe the first two or three

241
00:08:57,318 --> 00:09:00,243
compartments, which the ship could survive.

242
00:09:00,423 --> 00:09:04,572
By turning, the Titanic opened more compartments

243
00:09:04,572 --> 00:09:05,868
along its side.

244
00:09:06,048 --> 00:09:08,661
The turn was the correct reaction.

245
00:09:08,841 --> 00:09:10,693
It just happened to be the worst

246
00:09:10,693 --> 00:09:12,660
possible way to meet that iceberg.

247
00:09:13,010 --> 00:09:16,252
Why couldn't the Titanic's middle propeller go

248
00:09:16,252 --> 00:09:17,027
in reverse?

249
00:09:17,207 --> 00:09:19,685
The ship had three propellers,

250
00:09:19,685 --> 00:09:22,081
the same as its twin Olympic,

251
00:09:22,081 --> 00:09:22,907
seen here.

252
00:09:23,087 --> 00:09:25,415
The small one in the middle is

253
00:09:25,415 --> 00:09:26,036
the key.

254
00:09:26,216 --> 00:09:28,731
The two outer ones were driven by

255
00:09:28,731 --> 00:09:30,254
giant steam engines,

256
00:09:30,254 --> 00:09:32,311
each as tall as a building.

257
00:09:32,491 --> 00:09:34,897
The center one was driven by a

258
00:09:34,897 --> 00:09:38,023
turbine that ran on leftover steam from

259
00:09:38,023 --> 00:09:41,069
those engines, a clever way to squeeze

260
00:09:41,069 --> 00:09:43,475
more power from the same coal.

261
00:09:43,655 --> 00:09:45,985
But that turbine could only turn one

262
00:09:45,985 --> 00:09:46,244
way.

263
00:09:46,424 --> 00:09:47,889
To stop or reverse,

264
00:09:47,889 --> 00:09:50,975
the center propeller simply had to stop.

265
00:09:51,325 --> 00:09:53,834
And the center propeller sat right in

266
00:09:53,834 --> 00:09:55,191
front of the rudder.

267
00:09:55,371 --> 00:09:58,401
Its water flow helps a rudder bite.

268
00:09:58,581 --> 00:10:01,744
According to testimony from the inquiries,

269
00:10:01,744 --> 00:10:04,455
the engines may have been ordered to

270
00:10:04,455 --> 00:10:07,091
stop or reverse as the ship turned.

271
00:10:07,271 --> 00:10:08,375
If that happened,

272
00:10:08,375 --> 00:10:11,425
the center propeller stopped pushing water over

273
00:10:11,425 --> 00:10:13,437
the rudder at the worst moment,

274
00:10:13,437 --> 00:10:15,189
and the turn became weaker.

275
00:10:15,369 --> 00:10:18,777
Historians still debate the exact orders,

276
00:10:18,777 --> 00:10:21,604
but the design made this possible.

277
00:10:21,954 --> 00:10:24,194
The mixed system was not a mistake.

278
00:10:24,374 --> 00:10:25,938
It was more efficient,

279
00:10:25,938 --> 00:10:28,070
and it was used on Olympic and

280
00:10:28,070 --> 00:10:29,065
Britannic too.

281
00:10:29,245 --> 00:10:31,733
Each design choice on a ship has

282
00:10:31,733 --> 00:10:34,066
a price, and this one was paid

283
00:10:34,066 --> 00:10:37,954
in maneuverability, something a liner almost never

284
00:10:37,954 --> 00:10:38,498
needed.

285
00:10:38,678 --> 00:10:39,793
Almost never.

286
00:10:39,973 --> 00:10:43,106
Engineers today still talk about this when

287
00:10:43,106 --> 00:10:46,314
they study how propulsion and steering work

288
00:10:46,314 --> 00:10:46,985
together.

289
00:10:47,335 --> 00:10:50,225
Why did the Titanic's lookouts have no

290
00:10:50,225 --> 00:10:51,062
binoculars?

291
00:10:51,242 --> 00:10:52,481
The crow's nest,

292
00:10:52,481 --> 00:10:54,494
high up on the front mast,

293
00:10:54,494 --> 00:10:57,360
was where two men watched for danger.

294
00:10:57,540 --> 00:11:00,037
But the binoculars were locked in a

295
00:11:00,037 --> 00:11:02,035
box, and the key was with an

296
00:11:02,035 --> 00:11:03,534
officer, David Blair,

297
00:11:03,534 --> 00:11:05,817
who was removed from the crew at

298
00:11:05,817 --> 00:11:08,457
the last minute before the voyage and

299
00:11:08,457 --> 00:11:10,741
left with the key in his pocket.

300
00:11:10,921 --> 00:11:13,910
Lookout Frederick Fleet later testified that with

301
00:11:13,910 --> 00:11:16,594
binoculars, they could have seen the iceberg

302
00:11:16,594 --> 00:11:18,119
in time to get out of the

303
00:11:18,119 --> 00:11:18,363
way.

304
00:11:18,713 --> 00:11:20,729
That claim is still debated,

305
00:11:20,729 --> 00:11:24,186
because binoculars narrow your view and lookouts

306
00:11:24,186 --> 00:11:26,851
were trained to scan with their eyes.

307
00:11:27,031 --> 00:11:29,237
And the night itself was against them.

308
00:11:29,417 --> 00:11:30,637
There was no moon,

309
00:11:30,637 --> 00:11:32,738
and the sea was perfectly calm.

310
00:11:32,918 --> 00:11:35,701
Normally, small waves break at the base

311
00:11:35,701 --> 00:11:37,984
of an iceberg and create a white

312
00:11:37,984 --> 00:11:39,268
line you can spot.

313
00:11:39,448 --> 00:11:41,682
That night, there were no waves at

314
00:11:41,682 --> 00:11:41,944
all.

315
00:11:42,124 --> 00:11:44,695
The iceberg was a dark shape on

316
00:11:44,695 --> 00:11:45,607
a dark sea.

317
00:11:45,958 --> 00:11:47,535
In two thousand seven,

318
00:11:47,535 --> 00:11:49,900
the key to that binocular box was

319
00:11:49,900 --> 00:11:52,982
sold at auction for ninety thousand pounds.

320
00:11:53,162 --> 00:11:55,632
It is sometimes called the key that

321
00:11:55,632 --> 00:11:57,678
could have saved the Titanic.

322
00:11:57,858 --> 00:12:00,644
The real lesson is less dramatic.

323
00:12:00,824 --> 00:12:02,840
Modern ships use radar,

324
00:12:02,840 --> 00:12:05,996
which sees through darkness and fog,

325
00:12:05,996 --> 00:12:08,713
and no one relies only on human

326
00:12:08,713 --> 00:12:11,079
eyes and a small box with a

327
00:12:11,079 --> 00:12:11,517
lock.

328
00:12:11,867 --> 00:12:14,250
Why didn't the closest ship come to

329
00:12:14,250 --> 00:12:15,408
save the Titanic?

330
00:12:15,588 --> 00:12:18,325
The Titanic had one of the most

331
00:12:18,325 --> 00:12:20,356
powerful radios at sea,

332
00:12:20,356 --> 00:12:22,916
run by two Marconi operators,

333
00:12:22,916 --> 00:12:25,654
Jack Phillips and Harold Bride.

334
00:12:25,834 --> 00:12:28,668
But they worked for the Marconi company,

335
00:12:28,668 --> 00:12:31,360
and their main job was sending private

336
00:12:31,360 --> 00:12:33,415
messages for rich passengers.

337
00:12:33,595 --> 00:12:36,843
That day, the Titanic received several ice

338
00:12:36,843 --> 00:12:38,854
warnings from other ships.

339
00:12:39,034 --> 00:12:41,155
At least one never made it to

340
00:12:41,155 --> 00:12:41,960
the bridge.

341
00:12:42,310 --> 00:12:45,393
The ship Californian was stopped in the

342
00:12:45,393 --> 00:12:48,398
ice, close enough to see the Titanic's

343
00:12:48,398 --> 00:12:51,482
lights, maybe ten to twenty miles away.

344
00:12:51,662 --> 00:12:54,648
Its radio operator tried to warn the

345
00:12:54,648 --> 00:12:56,473
Titanic about the ice,

346
00:12:56,473 --> 00:13:00,040
and Phillips, busy with passenger messages,

347
00:13:00,040 --> 00:13:02,612
cut him off with a message that

348
00:13:02,612 --> 00:13:04,520
basically said shut up,

349
00:13:04,520 --> 00:13:05,350
I am busy.

350
00:13:05,530 --> 00:13:06,726
A little later,

351
00:13:06,726 --> 00:13:10,476
the Californian's operator turned off his radio

352
00:13:10,476 --> 00:13:11,753
and went to bed.

353
00:13:11,933 --> 00:13:15,660
Minutes later, the Titanic hit the iceberg.

354
00:13:15,840 --> 00:13:18,120
Its distress calls went to a ship

355
00:13:18,120 --> 00:13:20,124
that was no longer listening.

356
00:13:20,474 --> 00:13:23,788
The Carpathia, about fifty-eight miles away,

357
00:13:23,788 --> 00:13:26,651
heard the call and raced north through

358
00:13:26,651 --> 00:13:27,254
the ice.

359
00:13:27,434 --> 00:13:30,048
It arrived around four in the morning,

360
00:13:30,048 --> 00:13:31,974
more than an hour and a half

361
00:13:31,974 --> 00:13:33,831
after the Titanic was gone.

362
00:13:34,011 --> 00:13:35,491
After the disaster,

363
00:13:35,491 --> 00:13:38,450
new rules required ships to keep their

364
00:13:38,450 --> 00:13:41,488
radios staffed twenty-four hours a day.

365
00:13:41,668 --> 00:13:44,102
A radio is only a safety device

366
00:13:44,102 --> 00:13:45,987
if someone is listening.

367
00:13:46,337 --> 00:13:48,865
Why did the Titanic have so few

368
00:13:48,865 --> 00:13:49,680
lifeboats?

369
00:13:49,860 --> 00:13:51,495
It carried twenty boats,

370
00:13:51,495 --> 00:13:54,288
enough for about one thousand one hundred

371
00:13:54,288 --> 00:13:57,625
seventy-eight people, with more than two thousand

372
00:13:57,625 --> 00:13:59,055
two hundred on board.

373
00:13:59,235 --> 00:14:00,884
And it was perfectly legal.

374
00:14:01,064 --> 00:14:04,512
British rules from the eighteen nineties based

375
00:14:04,512 --> 00:14:07,285
the number of lifeboats on the ship's

376
00:14:07,285 --> 00:14:10,057
tonnage, and the scale stopped at ten

377
00:14:10,057 --> 00:14:11,107
thousand tons.

378
00:14:11,287 --> 00:14:13,765
The Titanic was more than four times

379
00:14:13,765 --> 00:14:16,657
bigger, and it actually carried more boats

380
00:14:16,657 --> 00:14:18,171
than the law required.

381
00:14:18,522 --> 00:14:21,451
The davits, the cranes that lowered the

382
00:14:21,451 --> 00:14:24,305
boats, were designed so each one could

383
00:14:24,305 --> 00:14:25,882
handle several boats.

384
00:14:26,062 --> 00:14:29,476
One designer had proposed far more lifeboats.

385
00:14:29,656 --> 00:14:32,304
But the deck space was kept open

386
00:14:32,304 --> 00:14:34,455
for passengers to walk on,

387
00:14:34,455 --> 00:14:35,944
and in those days,

388
00:14:35,944 --> 00:14:38,510
lifeboats were seen as ferries,

389
00:14:38,510 --> 00:14:41,075
used to move people to a rescue

390
00:14:41,075 --> 00:14:43,971
ship, not to hold everyone at once.

391
00:14:44,151 --> 00:14:45,196
On the night,

392
00:14:45,196 --> 00:14:47,367
some boats left half empty.

393
00:14:47,547 --> 00:14:49,907
The first boat lowered had room for

394
00:14:49,907 --> 00:14:52,672
sixty-five people, and it left with about

395
00:14:52,672 --> 00:14:53,549
twenty-eight.

396
00:14:53,899 --> 00:14:56,418
About fifteen hundred people died.

397
00:14:56,598 --> 00:14:58,374
In nineteen fourteen,

398
00:14:58,374 --> 00:15:02,519
the first International Convention for the Safety

399
00:15:02,519 --> 00:15:03,787
of Life at Sea,

400
00:15:03,787 --> 00:15:08,016
called SOLAS, required lifeboat space for everyone

401
00:15:08,016 --> 00:15:11,230
on board, plus lifeboat drills and the

402
00:15:11,230 --> 00:15:14,529
ice patrol that still watches the North

403
00:15:14,529 --> 00:15:15,290
Atlantic.

404
00:15:15,470 --> 00:15:17,757
The Titanic did not just sink.

405
00:15:17,937 --> 00:15:20,236
It rewrote the rules of the sea.

406
00:15:20,586 --> 00:15:23,604
Why did the Titanic break in two?

407
00:15:23,784 --> 00:15:25,035
As the bow sank,

408
00:15:25,035 --> 00:15:27,536
the stern rose out of the water,

409
00:15:27,536 --> 00:15:29,803
until a huge part of the ship

410
00:15:29,803 --> 00:15:31,600
was hanging in the air,

411
00:15:31,600 --> 00:15:32,538
unsupported.

412
00:15:32,718 --> 00:15:35,208
The hull was never designed to carry

413
00:15:35,208 --> 00:15:35,899
that load.

414
00:15:36,079 --> 00:15:37,157
Near the end,

415
00:15:37,157 --> 00:15:38,897
the ship broke apart,

416
00:15:38,897 --> 00:15:42,627
roughly between the third and fourth funnels.

417
00:15:42,807 --> 00:15:46,458
The upper structure even had expansion joints,

418
00:15:46,458 --> 00:15:49,315
small built-in gaps that let it flex

419
00:15:49,315 --> 00:15:50,029
in waves.

420
00:15:50,209 --> 00:15:52,403
Some researchers think one of them was

421
00:15:52,403 --> 00:15:54,423
a weak point where the break began.

422
00:15:54,773 --> 00:15:56,643
For seventy-three years,

423
00:15:56,643 --> 00:15:59,760
many people believed the Titanic sank in

424
00:15:59,760 --> 00:16:03,268
one piece, because the official inquiries did

425
00:16:03,268 --> 00:16:05,839
not conclude that it broke apart,

426
00:16:05,839 --> 00:16:08,879
even though some survivors said it did.

427
00:16:09,059 --> 00:16:11,284
Then, in nineteen eighty-five,

428
00:16:11,284 --> 00:16:14,697
Robert Ballard's expedition found the wreck at

429
00:16:14,697 --> 00:16:17,812
about three thousand eight hundred meters,

430
00:16:17,812 --> 00:16:20,483
more than twelve thousand feet deep.

431
00:16:20,663 --> 00:16:23,138
The bow and stern were lying about

432
00:16:23,138 --> 00:16:24,959
six hundred meters apart.

433
00:16:25,139 --> 00:16:27,054
The survivors had been right.

434
00:16:27,405 --> 00:16:29,675
The bow sank at an angle and

435
00:16:29,675 --> 00:16:32,432
plowed into the mud almost intact.

436
00:16:32,612 --> 00:16:34,831
The stern spun as it fell and

437
00:16:34,831 --> 00:16:35,979
was torn apart.

438
00:16:36,159 --> 00:16:38,138
Once the ship broke,

439
00:16:38,138 --> 00:16:39,920
the end came fast.

440
00:16:40,100 --> 00:16:42,406
At two twenty in the morning,

441
00:16:42,406 --> 00:16:44,315
the Titanic disappeared.

442
00:16:44,495 --> 00:16:46,933
Two hours and forty minutes after a

443
00:16:46,933 --> 00:16:49,441
scrape, it was resting on the bottom

444
00:16:49,441 --> 00:16:50,555
of the Atlantic.

445
00:16:50,905 --> 00:16:52,414
So here are the ten.

446
00:16:52,594 --> 00:16:54,626
Walls that stopped too low.

447
00:16:54,806 --> 00:16:56,286
A double bottom,

448
00:16:56,286 --> 00:16:57,859
but single sides.

449
00:16:58,039 --> 00:16:59,955
Rivets that may have popped,

450
00:16:59,955 --> 00:17:02,760
and steel that turned brittle in freezing

451
00:17:02,760 --> 00:17:03,171
water.

452
00:17:03,351 --> 00:17:05,920
A narrow rudder and a propeller that

453
00:17:05,920 --> 00:17:07,205
could not reverse.

454
00:17:07,385 --> 00:17:09,537
Lookouts without binoculars,

455
00:17:09,537 --> 00:17:11,381
a radio nobody answered,

456
00:17:11,381 --> 00:17:13,226
lifeboats for only half,

457
00:17:13,226 --> 00:17:15,454
and a hull that broke in two.

458
00:17:15,634 --> 00:17:18,177
No single part sank the Titanic.

459
00:17:18,357 --> 00:17:19,622
They did it together.

460
00:17:19,973 --> 00:17:22,693
Every ship you board today carries a

461
00:17:22,693 --> 00:17:25,262
piece of the Titanic in its rules,

462
00:17:25,262 --> 00:17:28,435
from lifeboats for everyone to radios that

463
00:17:28,435 --> 00:17:29,342
never sleep.

464
00:17:29,522 --> 00:17:32,404
If you want more machines explained part

465
00:17:32,404 --> 00:17:35,141
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

466
00:17:35,321 --> 00:17:37,161
And tell us in the comments which

467
00:17:37,161 --> 00:17:39,001
machine you want us to take apart

468
00:17:39,001 --> 00:17:39,280
next.
